"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[52091],{652091:e=>{e.exports="# How to find metrics\n\n## Tool: apiGetLakewatchOverviewMetrics\n\nUse **`apiGetLakewatchOverviewMetrics`** to fetch one of four summary metric types. It takes:\n\n- **`thoughts`** — Short reasoning for why you are fetching this metric.\n- **`metricType`** — One of: `datasource`, `security-case`, `system-case`, `observable`.\n\n### metricType → What you get\n\n| metricType        | Purpose                                                                                                                         | Warehouse required? |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| **datasource**    | Datasource count, per-datasource health/status, rollup metrics, event counts.                                                   | Yes                 |\n| **security-case** | Security case summary: active case counts, new in 7d/24h, unassigned. Active = OPEN, IN_PROGRESS, MITIGATED, STATE_UNSPECIFIED. | No                  |\n| **system-case**   | System case summary: same (active case counts, new in 7d/24h, unassigned).                                                      | No                  |\n| **observable**    | Top 10 observables by kind (e.g. table, column).                                                                                | Yes                 |\n\n### When to use each\n\n- **“How many security cases?” / “Security case summary” / “Active security cases” / “Unassigned cases”** — Use `metricType: 'security-case'`. The response includes active case counts (OPEN, IN_PROGRESS, MITIGATED, STATE_UNSPECIFIED), new in 7d/24h, unassigned.\n- **“How many system cases?” / “System case summary” / “Active system cases”** — Use `metricType: 'system-case'`. Same stats; active cases are OPEN, IN_PROGRESS, MITIGATED, STATE_UNSPECIFIED.\n- **“Datasource health” / “How are my datasources?” / “Datasource counts or metrics”** — Use `metricType: 'datasource'`. Requires a selected SQL warehouse; if none is selected, the tool returns a note saying so.\n- **“Top observables” / “Most active observables”** — Use `metricType: 'observable'`. Also requires a selected SQL warehouse.\n\n### Warehouse requirement\n\n- **datasource** and **observable**: Require a selected SQL warehouse in Lakewatch. If the user has not selected a warehouse, the tool returns a note; inform the user they can select a warehouse to see these metrics.\n- **security-case** and **system-case**: No warehouse required.\n\n## Combining with other tools\n\n- For **filtered lists** (e.g. “list open cases” or “cases for rule X”), use **apiListLakewatchSecurityCases** (or **apiListLakewatchSystemCases** for system cases) with the appropriate filters; those return paginated items and optional total/cursor, not the high-level summary.\n- For **a single resource’s details**, use **apiGetLakewatchResourceById** with the resource type and ID.\n- For **navigating** to the Home page or a list/detail page, use the **navigation** skill and **navigateWithinLakewatch**.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Health, counts, summary metrics, security case/system case summaries** — Use **apiGetLakewatchOverviewMetrics** with the right `metricType` (`datasource` | `security-case` | `system-case` | `observable`).\n- **datasource** and **observable** require a selected SQL warehouse; **security-case** and **system-case** do not.\n- For filtered lists of resources, use the **list** tools; for navigation use **how-to-navigation**.\n"}}]);
